## 1. Running the incident

### Pre-flight (before the room starts)

```bash
source ~/argo-lab-env.sh
reset-lab.sh CP-capstone --verify-only --local     # expect 23 PASS rows
```

**Every learner must be at `CP-capstone` before injection.** A learner who starts from a broken Lab 5 will chase their own leftover mistake as if it were an injected fault. Drop `--local` on the standard learner VM image.

### Inject

```bash
inject-capstone-faults.sh inject all          # order: F1 F2 F3 F6 F4 F5 F7
inject-capstone-faults.sh verify all          # every fault must report PRESENT
inject-capstone-faults.sh status              # which markers exist
```

Injection takes a few minutes: F5 waits for Kubernetes to mint a token, F7 waits for the doomed ReplicaSet to crash.

> 🔴 **F5 will report `ABSENT`, and re-running the injector will not fix it.** Deleting and recreating a *legacy* ServiceAccount token Secret under the same name mints a **byte-identical** token, so the stored copy never goes stale. Without a workaround the class gets **six** faults, not seven.
>
> **Apply the tested workaround in section 0.2 of [the verified walkthrough](../../instructor/day-2/capstone-restore-platform-SOLUTION.md)** immediately after `inject all`. It deletes and recreates the ServiceAccount itself, which changes its UID and therefore the token, and it is verified not to undo F4.

> 💡 **Verify before you announce the start.** Any other fault reporting `ABSENT` means one seventh of the exercise is missing — re-run `inject-capstone-faults.sh inject <Fn>` for that one.

### During

```bash
capstone-check.sh --instructor     # adds the failing signal per unresolved area
```

🔴 **Never run `--instructor` on a projector or a learner's machine during the capstone.** It names the failing signal, which hands them the triage.

### Reset and cleanup

```bash
inject-capstone-faults.sh revert all          # reverse order: F7 F5 F4 F6 F3 F2 F1
inject-capstone-faults.sh verify all          # every fault should now report ABSENT
capstone-check.sh                             # expect all six areas resolved, exit 0
```

If a learner's environment is beyond repair (for example they force-pushed, or hand-removed a finalizer):

```bash
reset-lab.sh CP-capstone --local              # rebuilds the checkpoint, DISCARDS the incident
inject-capstone-faults.sh inject all          # re-inject if they want to continue
```

**Order matters in `revert all`,** and the scripts enforce it: F3 restores Git *before* deleting its strays (otherwise the root recreates them), and the two storefront-gitops faults revert newest-first so the recorded SHAs stack correctly.

