## 2. What `capstone-check.sh` actually measures

Useful when a learner insists an area is fixed and the checker disagrees.

| Area | Resolved when… | Which fault it catches |
|---|---|---|
| argo cd platform components | `argocd-repo-server` and `argocd-server` have available replicas, and the controller StatefulSet has ready replicas | F7 |
| workload cluster connectivity | the workload API answers **and** the `bearerToken` stored in `cluster-workload` equals the live `argocd-manager-token` | F5 |
| application source rendering | no Application carries a `ComparisonError` condition | F1 (and F7 while it is down) |
| application generation and ownership | no Application outside the expected eight, and no `SharedResourceWarning` | F2, F3 |
| deployment policy (permissions) | no Application's last operation is `Failed` with a message matching `forbidden`/`denied`/`RBAC` | F4 |
| workload runtime health | all eight expected Applications are `Synced` **and** `Healthy` | F6 (and the tail of every other fault) |

> **The connectivity check compares the stored token with the live one byte for byte.** A learner who "fixes" F5 by writing a Secret by hand with the wrong token will stay unresolved with everything looking fine in the UI. Point them at `argocd cluster get workload`.

