### F3 — 👥 A second root: two owners for one Deployment

| | |
|---|---|
| **Intended symptom** | Two unexpected Applications — `team-root` and `platform-agent-dup` — plus a `SharedResourceWarning`, and a tracking annotation on the agent Deployment that changes between readings |
| **Actual root cause** | A commit added `apps/team-root.yaml` (a second root, which `platform-root` therefore creates) and `team-root/platform-agent-dup.yaml` (a child pointing at **the same source and destination** as the legitimate `platform-agent`) |
| **Why the symptom misleads** | `platform-root` itself stays 🟢 `Synced`/`Healthy` throughout — it is doing exactly what Git says. Learners reading the parent conclude the App-of-Apps tree is fine. **A healthy parent does not automatically mean every child is healthy** |

**First safe command**

```bash
argocd app get platform-root -o tree
```

**Evidence to expect**

- The tree shows a child `team-root` that is not in the known-good inventory.
- `team-root` in turn owns `platform-agent-dup`.
- `platform-agent` and `platform-agent-dup` have identical `spec.source` and `spec.destination`.
- A `SharedResourceWarning` condition, and a `tracking-id` annotation on the agent Deployment that differs between two readings a minute apart.
- `git log` shows **"platform: add team-root app-of-apps"**.

**Owner of the problem:** the `platform-config` repository — the child Application **files** under the path `platform-root` syncs.

**Correct repair — order is the lesson**

```bash
# A — remove the REASON first
cd ~/capstone/platform-config && git pull --ff-only
git log -n 10 --format='%h %an %s'; git show <sha> --stat
git revert <sha> && git push
argocd app get platform-root --refresh

# D — then remove the objects, WITHOUT cascade
argocd app delete team-root          --cascade=false
argocd app delete platform-agent-dup --cascade=false
```

> 🔴 **The safety point of this fault.** `platform-agent-dup` shares a live Deployment with `platform-agent`. **A cascading delete removes that Deployment** — a real outage caused by the repair. Make the room say the cascade decision out loud before anyone presses enter.

**Why tempting alternatives are wrong**

| Tempting fix | Why it is wrong |
|---|---|
| Deleting the child first | `team-root` has self-heal on and recreates it within a minute. This is the "my fix reverted" moment — let them experience it once, briefly |
| Deleting `team-root` with cascade | Prunes `platform-agent-dup`, which takes the shared Deployment with it |
| Adding `ignoreDifferences` to silence the flapping annotation | Hides a genuine ownership conflict |
| `kubectl delete` on the duplicate Application | Uncontrolled, and skips the finalizer that makes cascade behaviour explicit |

**Verification**

```bash
argocd app list                                   # exactly 8
argocd app get platform-root -o tree              # three children, no team-root
kubectl --context k3d-workload -n platform-system get deploy    # the agent is still running
kubectl --context k3d-mgmt -n argocd get applications \
  -o custom-columns='NAME:.metadata.name,TRACKED:.metadata.annotations.argocd\.argoproj\.io/tracking-id'
# read twice, two minutes apart: the owner must not change
```

**Cleanup / reset:** `revert F3` restores Git first, then orphan-deletes both objects, retrying until they are really gone.

**Common learner misconceptions**

- *"The root is healthy, so the tree is fine."*
- *"Deleting the Application deletes only the Application."* Only with `--cascade=false`. The default takes the workload too.
- *"Two Applications managing one resource is harmless because the content is identical."* It is a flapping owner, a shared-resource warning, and a deletion hazard.

**Hint sequence to give**

1. *"Count your Applications and compare with the inventory. Which ones do you not recognise?"*
2. *"Ask who wrote each one. What is the difference between an owner reference and a tracking ID?"*
3. *"If a root creates a child from a file, what has to change for the child to stop existing — and what happens if you delete the child first?"*

**When to reveal:** after **0:55**. Reveal the **cascade danger immediately** and unprompted if anyone is typing a delete without `--cascade=false`.

