### F5 — 🗝️ Rotated token: 401 from the whole cluster

| | |
|---|---|
| **Intended symptom** | A sync fails with `failed to discover server resources for group version apps/v1: Unauthorized`. ⚠️ **`argocd cluster list` and the Clusters page keep reporting `Successful` throughout** |
| **Actual root cause** | The `argocd-manager-token` Secret on the workload cluster was deleted and recreated, so Kubernetes minted a **new** token. The `cluster-workload` Secret on the management cluster still carries the old one. Every call returns **401** |
| **Why the symptom misleads** | Three ways. **(1)** The place everyone checks — the Clusters page — says `Successful`, because the controller holds an already-authenticated watch and answers comparisons from cache. **(2)** The learner's own `kubectl` works perfectly: different identity. **(3)** There is no dramatic moment; Applications keep showing plausible statuses until something forces a *fresh* call |

**First safe command**

```bash
argocd app get <a-failing-app> --show-operation | grep -E "Phase|Message"
```

**Evidence to expect**

- An operation message containing **`Unauthorized`**, for example: `one or more synchronization tasks are not valid: failed to discover server resources for group version apps/v1: Unauthorized`.
- The same word in the controller log: `kubectl --context k3d-mgmt -n argocd logs statefulset/argocd-application-controller --since=10m | grep -i unauthorized`.
- ⚠️ **`argocd cluster list` says `Successful`.** Do not let a learner rule this fault out on that basis — and do not let them conclude the tool is broken. Use it: it is the best "a status is a measurement with a timestamp" moment in the course.
- The `argocd-manager-token` Secret on the workload cluster is **much younger** than the `cluster-workload` Secret on mgmt — ages tell the story without printing anything secret.

**Owner of the problem:** the platform team, in the cluster-registration Secret on the management cluster.

**Correct repair (path C)**

```bash
cd ~/capstone/platform-config
TOKEN="$(kubectl --context k3d-workload -n argocd-access get secret argocd-manager-token \
          -o go-template='{{ index .data "token" | base64decode }}')"
CA="$(kubectl --context k3d-workload -n argocd-access get secret argocd-manager-token \
      -o jsonpath='{.data.ca\.crt}')"
sed -e "s|<TOKEN>|${TOKEN}|" -e "s|<CA_DATA>|${CA}|" clusters/workload.secret.template.yaml \
  | kubectl --context k3d-mgmt apply -f -
unset TOKEN CA
```

> **Why the template and not a hand-written Secret?** The template carries the correct `server`, the five managed `namespaces`, and `clusterResources: "false"`. Re-rendering it restores the **scope** as well as the credential. This is also the only F5 repair the checker will accept, because it compares the stored token with the live one.

> **Note for this delivery:** Day 1 Lab 2 performs this step with a helper script, `module2-connection-helper.py`. **That helper is not present in this repository** (see the capstone revision report). The `sed` form above is the supported capstone path and works on both macOS and Ubuntu — it edits a stream, never a file in place.

**Why tempting alternatives are wrong**

| Tempting fix | Why it is wrong |
|---|---|
| Adding RBAC on the workload cluster | 401 is authentication. No Role will ever help |
| Re-registering with `argocd cluster add` | Would rewrite the Secret without the course's namespace scope and `clusterResources: "false"`, quietly widening Argo CD's reach |
| Restarting the application controller | A restart is an intervention, not a diagnosis. The credential is still stale afterwards |
| Deleting and re-creating Applications | Destroys evidence and fixes nothing |

**Verification**

```bash
argocd cluster get workload      # a FRESH attemptedAt — the word Successful alone proves nothing here
argocd app get <app> --show-operation | grep -i unauthorized   # expect nothing
```

> 🔁 **Tell the room to re-triage after this repair, and warn them the picture gets worse first.** Re-registering rebuilds the cluster cache with a fresh list call. In the verified run, **three Applications turned `Unknown`/`Missing` within a minute** — `storefront-prod-workload`, `platform-quotas`, `platform-netpol` — which is what finally reveals F4. Have them log those rows as "revealed after C*n*", not as damage they caused.

**Cleanup / reset:** `revert F5` mints a token if needed and re-registers the cluster from the template. An exact byte-for-byte restore is impossible because the original token was invalidated at injection.

**Common learner misconceptions**

- *"My `kubectl` works, so the credential is fine."* Different identity entirely.
- *"The Clusters page says Successful, so connectivity is fine."* **The most expensive misconception in the incident.** It is reporting the last connection it measured.
- *"401 means the cluster is down."* The cluster is up and healthy.
- *"I should widen the namespaces list so more works."* Nothing about scope is broken.

**Hint sequence to give**

1. *"Read the failure message word for word. Is that word `forbidden`, or is it `Unauthorized`?"*
2. *"What is the difference between 401 and 403, and which system says each one?"*
3. *"The management cluster stores a **copy** of a token that lives on the workload cluster. Compare the ages of the two Secrets. And ask why the Clusters page can still say `Successful`."*

**When to reveal:** after **0:45** — this fault masks two others and a room stuck on it runs out of time. Reveal earlier if more than half the room is stuck here at 0:40.

